{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "F59ScalarInsideAFaq",
    "description": "F59, EarthSciML/EarthSciAST#431: a variable with no `shape` -- a scalar `unknown` -- reads as NaN inside a `faq` expression. The scalar itself is right when observed directly; every aggregate that reads it is NaN, with no refusal and no warning. Found at PLAN.md §7.3.8, moving the pin to the toolchain that fixed F58.\n\n`base` is the sum of `v`, which is 10 and reads as 10. `s[k] = base + k` should be 11, 12, 13, 14 and is NaN, NaN, NaN, NaN. The two assertions below are the RIGHT answers, so this file fails today and goes green the day #431 does -- which is what `run-tests.sh`'s known-limitations stage reads.\n\nSCOPE, measured across four spellings of the same document: a `faq` with an empty `output_idx` read by bare name is NaN; the same read as `index(base)` with no subscript is NaN; a `const` scalar read by bare name is NaN; and a 1-long AXIS read as `index(base, 1)` is CORRECT. So it is the unshaped variable that is unreadable rather than the reduction that produced it, and a length-1 axis is the workaround.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN ITS ONE RED TEST. This repository has 65 unshaped `unknown`s across 12 components, and only `components/tank_temperature.esm` went red -- because a mounted component's inline tests are not run (F47), so a component that is only ever mounted cannot fail this way however many of its aggregates are now NaN. Every fixture and oracle is still green, which says the mounted paths are fine; it is not evidence, because the tests that would say so do not run.\n\nIT IS NOT A REGRESSION, though it was first reported as one. `esm simulate` answers NaN here on `8ac536bf0` too, and on every commit tested; what changed in `8ac536bf0..eec6a7d64` is that #412 gave the inline-test runner a retry that builds a state-free document through the build pipeline, so `esm test` began reaching a path it had never taken. The suite going green at the old pin was evidence about `esm test` and was read as evidence about the evaluator. EarthSciAST #420 and #423 are innocent either way. EarthSciAST #433 fixes it: a build-time field's rank comes from its declaration, so an unshaped variable materializes at `IxDyn(&[])`.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "probe"
    ]
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "rows": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 4
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "ScalarOperand": {
      "variables": {
        "v": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rows"
          ],
          "description": "1,2,3,4."
        },
        "base": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Their sum, 10, as a SCALAR."
        },
        "s": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rows"
          ],
          "description": "base + k, read by bare name."
        }
      },
      "equations": [
        {
          "lhs": "v",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "faq",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [
              "k"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "k": {
                "from": "rows"
              }
            },
            "expr": "k"
          }
        },
        {
          "lhs": "base",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "faq",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [],
            "ranges": {
              "k": {
                "from": "rows"
              }
            },
            "reduce": "+",
            "expr": {
              "op": "index",
              "args": [
                "v",
                "k"
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "lhs": "s",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "faq",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [
              "k"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "k": {
                "from": "rows"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "+",
              "args": [
                "base",
                "k"
              ]
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "a_scalar_is_readable_inside_a_faq",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "base",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 10.0
            },
            {
              "variable": "s",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 11.0,
              "coords": {
                "rows": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "s",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 14.0,
              "coords": {
                "rows": 4
              }
            }
          ],
          "description": "The right answers. base is 10 and s[k] is base + k, so 11 at the first row and 14 at the last. Today s is NaN throughout."
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
